Your scenario is very detailed and logical, but there are 3 major issues that
will cause duplicate data or data loss if not handled correctly.

Here is an analysis of your workflow and how to fix the conflicts between the
Background Process and the Clock-Out Action.

Issue 1: The "In-Out Pair" Logic Conflict

You said: "I only send data if the pair is complete (In and Out)."

The Risk: If the Background Process sees a complete pair (In/Out) and starts
sending it, and at the exact same time the user clicks the Clock-Out button,
both processes will try to send the same Location chunks and the same "Out"
entry.

The Solution: The "Syncing" Lock You must use a "Lock" variable that both the
background process and the UI share.

1.  When Background starts: if (isSyncing) return; isSyncing = true;
2.  When Clock-out starts: if (isSyncing) wait_or_skip; isSyncing = true;

Issue 2: Location Data Chunks & Deletion

You said: "If location sync is success, then remove data from Hive."

The Risk: If the API call for locations succeeds, but your app crashes before it
can delete the data from Hive, the data remains in Hive. Next time the sync
runs, it will send the same locations again.

The Solution: Server-Side Idempotency Since you are deleting data from Hive
(instead of just marking it is_synced), you must ensure the server can handle
duplicates.

  - The server should check the timestamp of each location point. If a location
    for that user at that exact timestamp already exists, the server should
    ignore it.

Issue 3: The "Cross-Day" Shift (27th to 28th)

You said: "Check date-wise in Hive... 27 date In and 28 date Out."

The Problem: If your background process only looks at the "Current Date" box, it
will miss the "In" from the 27th.

The Solution: Search by "Status," not just "Date" Instead of only checking
today's date box:

1.  The background process should look at the last 2 days of boxes.
2.  It should look for any entry where is_sync == false.
3.  If it finds an In on the 27th and an Out on the 28th, it links them together
    as one shift.

Recommended "Clock-Out" Sequence (To avoid conflict)

To make sure your manual Clock-out doesn't clash with the background process,
follow this code logic:

// 1. User clicks 'Clock Out'
// 2. SAVE the 'Out' entry to Hive immediately (is_sync: false)
// 3. STOP the background process worker (cancel the task)
// 4. CHECK if 'isSyncing' variable is true. 
//    If yes, wait for 2 seconds.

// 5. RUN THE SYNC SEQUENCE:
   // a. Find the 'In' entry. If is_sync == false, call Clock-In API.
   // b. If success, update Hive (is_sync = true).
   // c. Find all Location data for this shift.
   // d. Send in chunks of 500.
   // e. If success, DELETE locations from Hive.
   // f. Finally, call Clock-Out API.
   // g. If success, update Hive (is_sync = true).

// 6. RESTART background process worker.

